    <div class="content">
      <p>Most adherence figures quoted for oral anticancer therapies come from trial populations — closely monitored, well-resourced, following a strict protocol. Almost none of that resembles the patients I dispensed to. This post is a working note on why that gap matters, and why I think it's a legitimate research question rather than just a practice grievance.</p>

      <h2>What the guideline assumes</h2>
      <p>Clinical guidelines for oral anticancer regimens generally assume a patient who can attend follow-up, afford the medicine consistently, and understand and manage side effects without much support. Trial-derived adherence figures are built on that assumption too, because trial participants are, by design, more able to meet it.</p>

      <h2>What the registry can't see</h2>
      <p>Population-level cancer registries record diagnoses, treatments, and outcomes — but not, in most cases, whether a patient actually took their medicine as prescribed in between. That's the layer my pharmacy background sits in, and it's largely invisible to the datasets epidemiologists usually work with.</p>

      <p>My developing research question tries to sit in that gap: using dispensing records as a proxy for real-world adherence, and testing whether that proxy relates to outcomes captured at a population level. It's still a proposal, not a finding — but naming the gap clearly feels like the first real piece of research work I've done.</p>
