      <span class="post-tag">Coursework</span>
      <h1>A pharmacist's first month inside the epidemiology literature</h1>
      <div class="post-meta">4 Mar 2026</div>
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      <p>Pharmacy training gives you a very particular relationship with evidence: you learn to trust a randomised controlled trial, apply a dosing guideline, and move on. Epidemiology reads completely differently. A month into the literature for this MPhil, the thing that's taken longest to sit right is how comfortable epidemiologists are with uncertainty that never fully resolves — confidence intervals instead of cut-offs, hazard ratios instead of "does it work".</p>

      <h2>The vocabulary shift</h2>
      <p>Confounding was the first real stumbling block. In practice, if a patient on a particular regimen did badly, my instinct was to look at the regimen. Epidemiology insists I also ask what else is different about that patient — age, comorbidity, socioeconomic access to follow-up care — before I can say anything about the regimen itself. It's a more honest way of thinking, but it's slower, and slower doesn't come naturally after years of needing an answer before the next patient reaches the counter.</p>

      <h2>Where my background actually helps</h2>
      <p>Reading adherence studies, I keep noticing what the data can't see: the patient who split tablets because of cost, the one who stopped for a side effect they never reported. Epidemiological method gives me rigour I didn't have. Practice gives me a sense of where the data is probably lying to itself. Neither is enough alone.</p>
    </div>
