    <div class="post-header">
      <span class="post-tag">Biostatistics</span>
      <h1>First pass at survival analysis</h1>
      <div class="post-meta">19 Feb 2026</div>
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      <p>Survival analysis is the first statistics I've met that's genuinely built for the kind of data oncology produces: not everyone reaches the event (relapse, death, discontinuation) within the study window, and pretending they did — or just excluding them — throws away real information.</p>

      <h2>Censoring, finally clicking</h2>
      <p>I'd read the word "censoring" a dozen times before this week without it landing. What made it click: a patient who's alive and event-free at the end of the study isn't a "no" — they're an unfinished observation. Kaplan-Meier curves are built specifically to use that partial information instead of discarding it.</p>

      <h2>Why an average is the wrong question</h2>
      <p>My instinct from practice was to ask "what's the average time to X" — which is exactly the wrong question for time-to-event data with censoring involved. The right question is closer to "what proportion of patients are event-free by time T", which is a different shape of thinking entirely, and one I'm still building intuition for.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</main>

